    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>A Native American republic that wrote down its laws from 1808 and adopted a constitution with three branches in 1827.</li><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sequoyah">Sequoyah</a>’s syllabary spread literacy in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cherokee">Cherokee</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cherokee-phoenix">Cherokee Phoenix</a> of 1828 printed English and Cherokee side by side.</li><li>Forced west on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trail-of-tears">Trail of Tears</a> in 1838–1839, the Nation rebuilt at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tahlequah">Tahlequah</a> but lost its self-government when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oklahoma">Oklahoma</a> became a state in 1907.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cherokee">Cherokee</a> Stand Watie was the last Confederate general in the field to surrender, on 23 June 1865, more than two months after Lee’s surrender at Appomattox.</p></aside>
    </section>

